\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}EXPERIMENTS}{6}{section.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.1}Experimental Setup}{6}{subsection.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{manually }}{6}{section*.28}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{To validate the fidelity of our re-implementation, we ran it using GPT-3.5-turbo---the backbone model adopted in the original HITS paper---on the Cli project; the resulting branch coverage deviates by less than 2\% from the figures reported in the original paper, confirming the reliability of our re-implementation.}}{6}{section*.30}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Specifically, RQ1 adopts \textbf  {Qwen3-Coder-30B} (hereafter \textbf  {QC3-30B}) as the backbone, while RQ2 adopts \textbf  {Qwen3-Coder-30B} (hereafter \textbf  {QC3-30B}). The use of different models across RQs reflects the distinct focuses of each evaluation: RQ1 prioritizes generation quality with a larger model, whereas RQ2 isolates the contribution of individual components under a more controlled, cost-efficient setting.}}{6}{section*.31}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Specifically, both RQ1 and RQ2 adopt Qwen3-Coder-30B (hereafter QC3-30B) as the unified backbone model, enabling a controlled and cost-efficient comparison across all experimental conditions.}}{6}{section*.32}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{To evaluate the inherent logic reasoning and code understanding capabilities of the models, we adopt a restricted context strategy for all test generation tasks. Specifically, for each method under test (MUT), the prompt only includes the source code of its declaring class and the names of the associated import packages. No additional project-level information, such as dependency class implementations or global configuration files, is provided. This setup ensures that the performance of \textsc  {CogPath}{} and the baselines is assessed under a consistent and challenging information-constrained environment, reflecting a realistic scenario where cross-file dependencies may be complex or partially inaccessible.}}{6}{section*.33}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Dataset Metrics with \#MUTs}}{7}{table.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:dataset}{{1}{7}{Dataset Metrics with \#MUTs}{table.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The key hyperparameters of \textsc  {CogPath}{} are configured as follows: the maximum number of iterations $\textit  {maxIter}$ is set to \textbf  {[\,]}, the no-improvement tolerance $\mitLambda $ is set to \textbf  {[\,]}, the backward slicing trigger threshold $\mitbeta $ is set to \textbf  {[\,]}, and the target coverage $\mittau $ is set to \textbf  {[\,]\%}. All experiments start from an empty initial test suite.}}{7}{section*.34}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}RQ1: Effectiveness of Our Approach}{7}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{To answer RQ1, we compare \textsc  {CogPath}{} against three state-of-the-art LLM-based test generation frameworks---SymPrompt, HITS, and Panta---on 14 real-world Java projects from Defects4J (v2.0.1). The evaluation focuses on both line coverage (LC) and branch coverage (BC) of complex methods, with Cyclomatic Complexity $CC > 10$.}}{7}{section*.36}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{To answer RQ1, we compare \textsc  {CogPath}{} against three state-of-the-art baselines on the selected dataset.}}{7}{section*.37}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {4.2.1}Overall Performance}{7}{subsubsection.4.2.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{This demonstrates that the combination of constraint hints and backward slicing allows \textsc  {CogPath}{} to more effectively navigate complex control flows and satisfy cross-method preconditions, compared with baselines that rely solely on prompt engineering or CFG-based analysis. The improvement over SymPrompt is particularly substantial (\textbf  {+89.3\%}), reflecting the limitation of approaches that lack explicit program analysis. Gains over HITS and Panta are smaller yet consistent (\textbf  {+16.9\%} and \textbf  {+23.8\%}, respectively), confirming that \textsc  {CogPath}{}’s enhancements provide complementary benefits beyond existing path enumeration or context-aware prompting techniques.}}{7}{section*.38}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ Gains over the two strongest baselines---HITS (\textbf  {+10.2\%}) and Panta (\textbf  {+12.1\%})---confirm that jointly reducing irrelevant context and making implicit preconditions explicit addresses coverage gaps that iterative refinement and context-aware prompting alone cannot close. The margin widens substantially against SymPrompt (\textbf  {+23.3\%}), underscoring the necessity of explicit program analysis when navigating logic-heavy control flows. }}{7}{section*.39}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {4.2.2}Performance on High-Complexity Projects}{7}{subsubsection.4.2.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{For example:}}{7}{section*.40}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Gson (CC$_{avg}$ = 23.25, highest in the benchmark): BC of 41.24\%, while SymPrompt reaches only 3.59\%, highlighting the difficulty of context-unaware methods in handling complex branching logic.}}{7}{section*.41}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{JCore (CC$_{avg}$ = 16.33): BC improves from 18.51\% (HITS) to 35.54\% (\textsc  {CogPath}{}), a gain of 17\%, demonstrating that backward slicing effectively isolates branch-relevant statements.}}{7}{section*.42}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Jsoup (CC$_{avg}$ = 14.13): BC rises from 17.36\% (HITS) to 52.55\% (\textsc  {CogPath}{}), a 36.4\% improvement, underscoring the impact of constraint hints in constructing necessary object states to reach target branches.}}{7}{section*.43}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ For instance, on Gson (CC$_{avg}$ = 23.25, the highest in the benchmark), \textsc  {CogPath}{} achieves a BC of 41.24\% while SymPrompt reaches only 3.59\%; on JCore (CC$_{avg}$ = 16.33), BC rises from 18.51\% (HITS) to 35.54\%; and on Jsoup (CC$_{avg}$ = 14.13), BC improves from 17.36\% (HITS) to 52.55\%. These gains collectively demonstrate that reducing irrelevant context via backward slicing and surfacing implicit preconditions via constraint hints are both essential for navigating the dense branching logic and cross-method dependencies that characterize high-complexity projects. }}{7}{section*.44}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Coverage Across Approaches (Updated \textsc  {CogPath}{})}}{8}{table.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:coverage}{{2}{8}{Coverage Across Approaches (Updated \ourmethod {})}{table.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {4.2.3}Cases of Limited Improvement}{8}{subsubsection.4.2.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {4.2.4}Summary}{8}{subsubsection.4.2.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In summary, \textsc  {CogPath}{} demonstrates \textbf  {robust effectiveness} across 14 real-world projects, achieving significant gains over state-of-the-art baselines, particularly in \textbf  {high-complexity and logic-intensive methods}. The combined use of backward slicing and constraint hints enables the LLM to reach branches that are otherwise inaccessible to prompt-only or context-limited approaches.}}{8}{section*.45}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In a minority of projects where branches depend primarily on simple value conditions, existing tools remain competitive, highlighting opportunities for further refinement in slice granularity.}}{8}{section*.46}\protected@file@percent }
